fact WorkItem(id: "WI-980", description: some(value: "A RULE HEAD's binding depends on DECLARATION ORDER. It must not.\n\nMUST AGREE WITH docs/proposals/059-secondary-entries.md R6, which defines the\ncorrect behaviour; this ticket implements it. Do not re-decide the policy here --\nif the implementation cannot meet R6, amend 059 rather than diverging from it.\n\nTHE POLICY IS NOT IN QUESTION. kernel-language.md §\"A rule head functor is\nresolved, not declared\" (WI-896) already says it: the functor runs the ordinary\nladder and the rule contributes a clause to whatever it lands on; only when the\nladder finds NOTHING does the rule introduce the name, scoped where written. The\ndefect is WHEN \"the ladder finds nothing\" is evaluated -- measured, against a\nHALF-BUILT name table, so textual order decides the answer.\n\nMEASURED (resolving the goal after load; counts are answers):\n\n  namespace s2                      namespace s1\n    rule p(1)                         sort Rec\n    sort Rec                            entity Rec(n: Int64)\n      entity Rec(n: Int64)              rule p(2)\n      rule p(2)                       end\n    end                               rule p(1)\n  end                               end\n\n  s2.p(2)=1   s2.Rec.p=<no symbol>    s1.p(2)=0   s1.Rec.p(2)=1\n\nSame pair, opposite order, two different programs: one predicate with two\nclauses, versus two predicates with one clause each.\n\nNOT A COMPANION QUESTION -- the identical split occurs with `namespace Rec ...\nend` (a proposal-059 secondary entry) in place of the sort body: enclosing rule\nwritten before => joins; written after => mints `Rec.p`. Both spellings agree\nwith each other and disagree with themselves across orders.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS: this is the source-order dependence the WI-925/926 category-set\nwork exists to remove, in the one name kind that still has it. It also silently\ndecides whether a rule EXTENDS someone else's predicate -- and extension is\nnon-monotone: MEASURED, `rule q(0) :- not p(2)` has one answer, and adding a\nsingle `rule p(2)` from an inner scope takes it to zero. A true statement becomes\nfalse, and proofs discharged from it (WI-537/538/539/558) were verified against a\nknowledge base that no longer exists. Which of the two readings you get should not\nturn on where in the file someone put a line.\n\nREQUIRED BEHAVIOUR (059 R6): the binding is computed against the FINISHED\nprogram, never the scanned prefix -- scopes outermost-first, all of a scope's\nheads together. The two programs above must both give the FIRST row's answer\n(`p(2)=1`, no `Rec.p`), since in the finished program `p` does resolve from\ninside `Rec`. A scope wanting its own name where an enclosing one resolves\nDECLARES it, the remedy §WI-896 already prescribes.\n\nLIKELY MECHANISM, to confirm rather than assume: the mint decision is taken in\n`scan_rule` during scan_definitions sub-pass 1, i.e. while the table that\ndecides it is still being built. Every other name kind is order-free because\npass 1 defines all of them before any pass 2 runs (the WI-321 cross-file\nmutual-recursion invariant); a rule head escapes it by being introduced during\nthat pass. Note sub-pass 3 also registers unlabeled rule head-functor Goals --\nestablish which pass actually takes the decision before changing either.\n\nWATCH FOR: a circular case the fix must define rather than stumble into -- an\ninner and an outer head that would each introduce, with nothing else declaring\nthe name. Outermost-first makes the inner one see the outer and join; confirm\nthat is what lands.\n\nMIGRATION RISK, to MEASURE FIRST: today's join-vs-mint split is load-bearing\nsomewhere or it is not, and the answer decides the size of this ticket. Before\nchanging behaviour, count the places in stdlib / anthill-stl / examples /\nanthill-todo / fixtures where an inner rule head currently MINTS only because\nits enclosing predicate is declared later in the file -- each becomes a join and\nchanges meaning. If that count is not zero, the fix needs a migration, not just\na reorder of passes.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the two programs above load to the same KB. A test asserts, for BOTH\norders and BOTH spellings (sort body and secondary entry), the same predicate\nidentity and the same answer counts -- four rows, since a pair that agrees in\nonly one order measures nothing. Say at the test site which rows fail when the\nfix is backed out (the enclosing-rule-written-after rows do; the others pass\neither way, by design, as the control). cargo-test green via\nrustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
